Pneumaclara

Ingredients: TIMBA + MELANCHOLIC, NOSTALGIC & SAD + Rhythm Acoustic Guitar
Creative Catalyst: Invented by miners two kilometers underground, drumming on ventilation pipes.

Pneumaclara subverts the high-octane, celebratory nature of Cuban timba by slowing its frantic polyrhythms into a heavy, suffocating crawl that feels like oxygen running out. The aggressive horn stacks and virtuosic piano tumbaos are completely stripped away, replaced by a hyper-amplified nylon-string rhythm acoustic guitar that plays the syncopated, percussive clave pattern directly on its wooden body while fretting minor-ninth arpeggios. Over this dry, intimate acoustic bedrock, cavernous sub-bass hums simulate the crushing barometric pressure of the deep earth, punctuated by metallic resonance that sounds like cold steel struck in pitch darkness.

Genre DNA

   Tempo & Pulse: 78–88 BPM. A sludgy, half-time adaptation of the classic timba 2-3 son clave*, where the syncopation feels heavy and dragged, as if moving through dense underground air.
*   Harmonic Palette: Phrygian dominant and harmonic minor scales, saturated with diminished sevenths and unresolved minor ninths that evoke claustrophobia and longing.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Pneumatic Reverberation:* Vocal tracks and percussion are routed through impulse responses captured inside sealed metal industrial air shafts, creating an oppressive, ringing spatial echo.
       Sub-Frequency Tapping:* Muted acoustic guitar palm-thumps are pitch-shifted down two octaves and blended with sub-bass sine waves to mimic subterranean foot-falls.
       Oxygen-Bleed Gating:* A harsh noise gate triggered by a simulated respirator breath that cuts the instrumental tail abruptly on every fourth bar, leaving total, eerie silence.
*   Dynamics: Suffocatingly tense; builds do not explode into choruses, but rather "compress" as instruments pile up in a tight frequency range until the track suddenly decompressing into a desolate acoustic solo.

Scene & Origin Lore

Born two kilometers below the Atacama crust in the decommissioned shafts of San Sebastián, Pneumaclara was forged by shift workers who transformed galvanized air-vent pipes into makeshift percussion rigs during a six-day cave-in. To conserve headlamp batteries, they played in absolute dark, keeping time with the dull thud of their boots and the metallic ping of steel pipes, while a single acoustic guitar—smuggled down in a tool chest—kept the memory of daylight alive. When the survivors resurfaced, they brought the sound to the surface coal towns, dressing in soot-stained linen shirts, heavy iron-toed boots, and wearing silver dust masks pulled down to their necks like ascots. Pneumaclara shows take place in disused train tunnels and sub-basements, illuminated only by single miners' lamps, where audiences dance a slow, swaying version of the casino wheel, stepping in unison to the subterranean clatter.

Instruments Used

*   Primary Acoustic: Rhythm Acoustic Guitar (nylon-string, microphoned internally with contact pickups to capture skin friction and fret wear).
*   Percussion: Steel Ventilation Pipe Offcuts, Anvils, Sub-octave Pitched Kick Drums, Cast-Iron Claves.
*   Melodic & Ambient: Sub-bass Synthesizers, Prepared Upright Piano (stuffed with rubber dampers), Low-register Accordion.

